A||||1||ponya||Directional/Wiper stalk||||5||
Z||000000||ponya||11-01-2000||10:09 PM||||Had the classic problem with the windshield wipers cycling every time I used the directional signals. Got a brand new stalk from Rodney Dickman, put it in, put the fuse in and voila, works great except that the windshield wipers still cycled. Is there something else I should be looking at? The new stalk looks great, works great, but the problem remains. Any suggestions appreciated.<BR>Thanks,  Bob||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||11-01-2000||10:28 PM||||The stalk has nothing to do with the wiper problem.  The stalk acts as a stick to control both the turn signal and wiper switches which are located inside the steering column.  Some have fixed their wiper problem by replaced the pulse control board, but for me it has always been the wiper switch in the steering column.<P>I got a replacement switch from Advance Auto for about $55, G.P. Sorensen P/N DS170.  It is also available from PartsAmerica.com<P>The general steps replace are as follows (might not be in exact order):<BR>1) remove steering wheel<BR>2) remove lock plate<BR>3) pull turn signal stalk from switch (be careful of the cruise wiring<BR>4) remove ignition key tumbler ( be careful with key buzzer contact<BR>5) remove column cover to access pivot pin<BR>6) remove turn signal switch<BR>7) disconnect and remove wiper switch<P>This is much more time consuming than actually difficult.  The factory manual has good diagrams to aid in the repair.  I found it easier to remove the column from the car and do the repair on my workbench.<P>Let me know if you have any specific questions.  Replacing this switch solved an out of control wiper problesm on my daughter's 87 coupe.<P>Good luck.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
